  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t>0$ is given by $v(t)=3{t}^{2}-6t.$ \nAt time $t=2,$ the particle is located at the position $x=6.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=5?$",
  "worked_solution": "The position of the particle at $t=5$ is equal to its position at $t=2$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [2,5].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(5)=x(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{5}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(5) & =6+{\\int }_{2}^{5}(3{t}^{2}-6t)\\,\\text{dt} \\\\  & =6+{[{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}]}_{2}^{5} \\\\  & =6+([(5)^{3}-3(5)^{2}]-[(2)^{3}-3(2)^{2}]) \\\\  & =6+([125-75]-[8-12]) \\\\  & =6+(50-(-4)) \\\\  & =6+54 \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=5$ is $x=60.$",
